    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Chinese Brand AITO Plans 2027 European Launch\nAircraft Carrier Sailing in the Pacific Takes Control of Refuelling Drone 2K Miles Away in Illinois\n23 photos\nPhoto: U.S. Navy\nAbout a month ago, in mid-September, the U.S. Navy announced it has decided to give the Boeing-made MQ-25A Stingray drone the role of an uncrewed aerial refuelling tanker, the first time in the history of the world that such an aircraft is used in such a capacity. And it didn’t take long for the military branch to give us a taste of how that decision could reshape the future of naval aviation operations.\nThe Stingray has been in the works for a while now, and it was initially designed to fulfil the same roles most other drones of this size fulfill. You know, stuff like intelligence, surveillance, reconnaissance (ISR), and strike missions.\nAlthough those capabilities are still on the table, someone, somewhere, decided it would be a good idea to use it as an airborne refuelling tanker, and the Navy immediately saw the merits of that. Hence, the $562 million contract Boeing got back in September to deliver three MQ-25A Stingray aerial refuelling drones starting in 2029.\nUntil that happens, the aerospace giant and the Navy are hard at work putting the design through its paces. The latest gimmick is having one of these drones controlled by people located on the deck of an aircraft carrier, thousands of miles away.\nThe carrier in question is the Nimitz-class USS Ronald Reagan (CVN 76), which is presently on duty somewhere in the Pacific Ocean. From there, in what the Navy calls a historic U.S. naval aviation event, the ship was able to take control of and command an MQ-25A located at Boeing's facility in Mascoutah, Illinois.\nAccording to the available info, the distance between the two was, at the time the exercise was conducted, 2,100 miles (3,380 km). To make the connection work, Reagan’s Unmanned Air Warfare Center (UAWC) employed an MD-5C Ground Control Station and a series of low-Earth orbit satellites.\nNeither the Navy nor Boeing shared any details on how the exercise unfolded or what was achieved, but, assuming everything worked as planned, we just witnessed something that, again, could reshape naval aviation operations.\nThe Stingray is powered by a single Rolls-Royce AE 3007N engine, and it has been designed to fly for a distance of up to 580 miles (930 km), either autonomously or controlled from a carrier deck or inside fighter plane cockpits.\nIn the tanker configuration, it carries a 15,000-pound (6,800 kg) aerial refuelling store (ARS) and a boom that allows it to refuel airplanes the likes of the F/A-18 Super Hornets mid-flight. It has been selected for this role as the Navy seeks to relieve said Super Hornets from their buddy refuelling and allow them to act in the capacity they were designed for.\nDespite its new role, the drone still comes equipped with underwing hardpoints that can carry anti-ship missiles and can continue to be used as an ISR asset.\nThe Navy will continue to test the design in the coming months, using the seven test aircraft that are being built at the Boeing facilities in St. Louis, Missouri, and Mascoutah, Illinois. The company’s MidAmerica facility is involved in testing as well, and so will Naval Air Station Patuxent River in Maryland, where carrier qualification testing will take place.\nBoeing is set to deliver the first operational drone in 2029. In all, the MQ-25A program of record calls for 76 of these drones to be deployed, including four engineering examples and five demonstration units. They will all cost the Navy $13 billion.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nMQ-25A Stingray U.S. Navy Boeing drone\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Second Crew of Mars Astronauts NASA Locked Up for a Year Are Coming Out on October 31",
